"""Twenty moderate-detail freighter variants after the too-busy D01-D05 set."""
import hashlib
import json
from pathlib import Path
from rh_redesign_paths import TOPIC

OUT=TOPIC/'rh_freighter/concepts/round_08'

COMMON='''Use case: precise-object-edit. Design ONE NEW three-view RH JUNKER FREIGHTER variant. The user wants TWENTY genuinely different geometric designs at APPROXIMATELY HALF THE DETAIL DENSITY of IMAGE 1, the previous D-series. This sheet is one of those twenty. Think deeply about a concise, elegant ANGULAR INDUSTRIAL HULL SHAPE. Use a small number of substantial, well-fitted volumes with purposeful steps, cuts and one or two deep recesses. The design must look like an authentic Freelancer Rheinland freighter rebuilt by scavengers. The primary geometry and silhouette, not paint differences, distinguish this variant.

REFERENCE ROLES:
IMAGE 1: previous D-series freighter concept. Its dimensions, original cabin, high hump and mechanism remain useful, but it has TOO MANY hatches, grilles, repeated panel fields and nested outlines. Explicitly remove about half that visual subdivision. Freely recompose the main hull surfaces in the direction specified below; this is not a simple recolour or copying the same design with one removed vent. Keep meaningful medium-size depth and authored forms.
IMAGE 2: ACTUAL ORIGINAL RH pirate freighter, authoritative for ancestry and scale. Source flight bounds W 9.465, H 11.463, L 23.722; main cargo body about 6.3 wide, cockpit glazing about 3.6 wide. It is a tall, narrow cargo body with a raised hump, a low forward cabin and two rear engine modules. Stay near these overall proportions and original physical size. No fat transport, giant cargo pod, fighter or rounded ball.
IMAGE 3: IMMUTABLE original FREIGHTER cabin. Reuse its complete original appearance at unchanged size and position: sloping upper blue glazing, original pane divisions, opaque pale lower metal face with SEPARATE small rectangular blue window slots, narrow black/yellow trim, original chin and underside equipment block. Do not reshape, armour, cage or otherwise modify this cabin. No new cockpit, visor or continuous lower blue band. Fit all new hull transitions around the cabin; its real original geometry must be reused later.
IMAGE 4: close-up of the actually implemented RH pirate elite model, for construction quality only: well-seated recessed red panel fields, deep returns, thick closed hull surfaces and sharp plane changes. Transfer that practical craftsmanship, at restrained density. It does not authorize copying the elite's cockpit, wings, open frame or wheel.
IMAGE 5: the user's historical RH freighter concept art. Preserve the original's clear hierarchy of angular cargo mass, raised narrow hump, horizontal shoulders and separated compact propulsion. Its green paint and sketch cockpit do not replace the real pirate cabin in image 3. It reinforces clarity of form, not a primitive blockout aesthetic.

MEDIUM DETAIL BUDGET, roughly HALF of the previous D-series:
Make about 55-65 percent of visible hull area calm connected planes. Keep the design sophisticated through major geometry and a small number of meaningful medium-scale features. Replace clusters of small hatch panels with one larger designed donor hull field. Use approximately ONE large recessed cargo-door field per side, ONE smaller service recess, and ONE ventilation pocket where appropriate; do not repeat all three in every hull zone. The roof can have ONE substantial inset service/ventilation feature and a broad inset side field. A vent pocket has only THREE OR FOUR broad internal blades. Cylinder units get one substantial sliding collar and a simple inset endcap, not three concentric bezels, mini hatches and extra ring stacks. Each engine gets one meaningful shoulder transition, one service field and a clear exhaust lip. Do not keep every D-series panel, slot and lip just at smaller scale. No dense rivets, fine grid seams, tiny decals, dozens of bolts or uniform grunge. This is a visual target, not a numeric polygon budget.

GEOMETRIC QUALITY:
Develop four to six strong primary/secondary decisions in the hull profile, shoulder contour, lower cargo outline and hump interface. These MUST give this variant a distinct outline and volume balance. Keep crisp faceted wedges, stepped solid side housings, substantial closed-back recesses and well-joined donor sections. Preserve some actual relief and useful joints; do not reduce everything to an undecorated box with a roof cap. Continuous material around a recess is the hull itself, not an exterior decorative frame. At most a few broad donor sections can be physically offset or shaped differently. Subtle fixed-housing asymmetry is allowed when specified. Colour supports the geometry; it cannot be the main change.

SOURCE IDENTITY AND MECHANISMS:
Keep the prominent high RH freighter HUMP above a horizontal shoulder line, approximately the upper third of body height. It is a narrow closed cargo-roof arch built from broad authored facets, rising and falling fore and aft; not a huge dome, ball, exposed circular wheel or tiny low roof vent. Show enough substantial rectilinear cargo mass below it. The cabin connects through a short enclosed hull junction in its original position.
Each side has EXACTLY THREE retractable short closed-ended faceted CYLINDERS: large middle, smaller fore and aft, ONE row only. Six total. They extend laterally from thick solid hull cheeks, with simple sleeves and a plausible free withdrawal path. All three views show the SAME FLIGHT extension. Do not turn them into wheels, guns or side exhausts. Keep their main placements symmetrical even if fixed donor covers differ.
Two original-sized main engines at the AFT end, left/right at the SAME HEIGHT; use short CLOSED solid couplers. In true SIDE, their silhouettes overlap so ONE engine outline is visible. In TOP, show TWO separate engines. No third engine, vertically staggered pair or enormous bells.

HARD EXCLUSIONS:
NO exposed pipes, hoses, cables or conduits. NO exterior frame, truss, diagonal brace, cage, scaffold, separate ribs, hoops, straps, open rail or skeletal pylon. NO wings, fins, thin side paddles or claws/cranes. No random small machinery attached to the hull. No geometric rivets or tiny fastener fields. No uniform smoothing or rounded bevel on every edge. No integrated guns, turrets or missiles; a small empty flush equipment pad is acceptable. All exposed recesses have a closed back. Original cabin unchanged.

ART AND PAGE:
Refined Freelancer-era industrial concept art with confident ink edges and controlled marker/gouache plane shading on off-white paper. Restrained worn pale beige/grey hull, muted dark red recessed/donor fields, a little faded olive and dark cavities. Use sparse material wear, broad fields and only a few seam lines. Show an integrated dangerous scavenger craft, not a toy or glossy modern product. Landscape: large 3/4 front-side perspective on LEFT; true SIDE / FLIGHT upper right nose RIGHT; true TOP / FLIGHT lower right nose UP. Exactly three full uncropped views of the SAME design, same mass proportions, cabin, hump, panel locations and six cylinders. Technical views must be clear and consistent. All are flight pose. Only title specified below and the two view labels.

DISTINCT VARIANT ARCHITECTURE:
'''

VARIANTS=[
 ('M01','D01','Клиновидные корпуса','Скошенные торцы','A tall central cargo block with matching large oblique cuts on its FRONT and REAR ends, creating a clear elongated six-sided side silhouette. The narrow raised faceted hump sits inside a continuous low shoulder ledge. Keep the side drum cheek one coherent blunt wedge, with a single large lower cargo recess and one compact service field. Both end cuts are primary hull planes, not bolt-on armour. Engine and original cabin joins stay short and closed.'),
 ('M02','D02','Клиновидные корпуса','Высокое переднее плечо','One long solid side wedge rises toward a high angular FORWARD shoulder immediately behind the original cabin and falls toward the lower aft corner. Emphasize that diagonal mass boundary while keeping the roof hump separately raised and recognizable. The three sliding drums emerge along the midlevel of this continuous wedge. One long inset recess follows its lower edge, with only one simple hatch field. The ship stays narrow; no protruding forward ram or wings.'),
 ('M03','D03','Клиновидные корпуса','Тяжёлая корма','Concentrate the main fixed side-housing mass toward the REAR of the cargo body. A solid angled aft shoulder encloses the compact engine couplers, while the forward hull corner slopes inward toward the unchanged low cabin. The actual engines keep original size and equal height. This rear-heavy housing is part of the sealed cargo body, not an engine cage. Retain the high midship hump and six symmetrical sliding drums. Use one major cargo-door field plus one restrained upper vent.'),
 ('M04','D02','Клиновидные корпуса','Срезанное днище','A deep cargo hull whose LOWER outline has two substantial angled cuts fore and aft, producing a broad shallow chisel-like belly between them. The upper shoulder stays mostly horizontal and the narrow high hump rises above it. Shape the cylinder carrier as a thick planar side projection integrated into the middle body, with a clear dark closed-backed cargo recess below. One service panel and one roof inset only. No underbody external bed or frame.'),
 ('M05','D05','Клиновидные корпуса','Ломаная линия плеча','Create one distinctive stepped DIAGONAL change in the solid upper shoulder: a high short rear shoulder plane, an oblique transition, then a lower front shoulder plane behind the cabin. The faceted hump remains an independent high arched cargo-roof mass centred over the hold. Keep the side housings deep and closed with the original drum row. A single large cargo hatch and one upper vent follow the authored plane changes. No diagonal external beam; the diagonal is a continuous hull surface.'),
 ('M06','D03','Ступенчатые корпуса','Два корпусных яруса','Two clearly offset CLOSED hull tiers: a narrower upper cargo wall supporting the high hump and a deeper lower side mass carrying the three drums. Join them through a broad sloping return with ONE long shallow recess, not an open gap or frame. The top view must show the stepped width of these tiers. Keep just a large lower cargo hatch and one medium upper access panel. Maintain source overall width, height and length.'),
 ('M07','D01','Ступенчатые корпуса','Утопленная носовая стенка','Design a deep, angular FORWARD BULKHEAD recess behind the unchanged cabin, with a closed sloped back flowing into its short original mounting junction. Two broad closed hull corners of unequal cut angle define this recess, not detached pillars. The side wall stays calm with one cargo door and clean cylinder collars. The high narrow faceted hump starts behind this recessed front face. Strong frontal volume and depth, modest panel count.'),
 ('M08','D04','Ступенчатые корпуса','Кормовой уступ','A substantial rectangular step cuts back the REAR LOWER cargo-body silhouette, leaving a short high closed aft shoulder that feeds directly into the paired engine couplers. The step is a filled closed hull contour, not an empty lattice. Keep the front cargo wall tall and source-like with the original cabin nearby. High narrow hump has one broad roof inset. The side cheek and three drums remain continuous above one large lower recessed panel.'),
 ('M09','D04','Ступенчатые корпуса','Смещённые боковые объёмы','Moderate structural asymmetry in fixed bodywork: one side housing has its widest substantial shoulder section slightly forward, the other slightly aft. Both remain inside comparable source-size envelopes and preserve the same three drum locations and retraction paths. Make these shoulder offsets readable in TOP view and consistent with perspective. Unify them with the original central closed cargo body and high hump. Use only one large inset on each donor shell and one roof vent.'),
 ('M10','D05','Ступенчатые корпуса','Глубокий средний борт','A broad continuous CLOSED side recess occupies the middle cargo wall below the drum row. Its upper and lower return planes create a strong solid step in section; the surrounding corners merge into the body rather than becoming a cage around an opening. Place one simple large cargo-door panel at its recessed back. The original hump stays high and narrowly extruded, with mostly calm facets. Engine collars and source cabin junction have just one meaningful secondary step each.'),
 ('M11','D01','Горб и плечи','Чёткий семигранный горб','Keep the original high arched roof mass, but give it a carefully proportioned SEVEN-PLANE longitudinal profile: broad rising and falling faces with a short crisp crown. Its transverse width is clearly narrower than the main hull. The side face has one large shallow inset field; roof has one concise service recess. Below it, retain a strong rectilinear cargo block with low solid drum cheeks and one cargo hatch. No stacked roof panels or raised arch ribs.'),
 ('M12','D05','Горб и плечи','Горб с крутой кормовой гранью','Make the preserved high roof hump subtly asymmetric FORE/AFT: a longer angled forward rise and a shorter steeper rear descending face, both still recognizably an arched RH freighter hump. Keep its crest over the central hold and its width narrow. Match this roof profile with a closed angular aft shoulder and a calmer planar front bulkhead behind the original cabin. Use one roof vent and one broad recessed side field; all six drums remain in the native arrangement.'),
 ('M13','D03','Горб и плечи','Короткая вершина','A tall faceted arch with a SHORT NARROW CROWN and long broad slopes into defined front/rear shoulder shelves. It must retain the original hump height and enclosing cargo volume, not become a flat cap or low wedge. The hull shoulders form two chunky closed planes at different fore/aft lengths, giving an intentional silhouette. Use one stepped cargo-door recess and one small service inset. Keep the six drums simple, with one collar each.'),
 ('M14','D02','Горб и плечи','Наклонные передние плечи','Two large solid sloping forward shoulders descend from the hump base toward the unchanged low original cabin, ending behind it. They are sealed parts of the cargo hull, not thin shields, wings or external braces. The hump itself stays high and narrow, with a clear ledge separating roof from shoulders. Keep a strong broad side cheek for the drums and one deep lower cargo hatch. One restrained inset vent is enough; shape and plane intersections dominate.'),
 ('M15','D05','Горб и плечи','Узкий верх и прямые борта','Emphasize the contrast between a relatively NARROW HIGH polygonal hump and straight deep cargo side walls, closely recalling the historical source. Rework the transition as two broad shallow oblique shoulder planes, fully closed, with a short forward stepped setback. No huge round dome. Side drum housings are compact angular extrusions of the main wall. Use one broad red cargo recess, one modest side service pocket and one roof inlet. Strong simple hierarchy with real medium-scale depth.'),
 ('M16','D04','Донорские сборки','Клин и ступенчатый кожух','Two physically different salvaged SIDE SHELLS: one is a blunt elongated wedge with a large diagonal end cut, the other a stepped rectangular casing with a deep single access recess. They have equal useful scale and preserve the same source drum positions and central cargo body. Make the asymmetry conspicuous in TOP while staying coherent. Roof hump remains original and high; original cabin untouched. One broad joining seam per donor section, no patchwork grid or numerous little hatches.'),
 ('M17','D02','Донорские сборки','Косой корпусной стык','A broad diagonal JOIN between two differently shaped solid donor hull sections gives the side its identity. One main section slopes outward slightly and the neighbouring section turns inward through a deep but narrow closed-backed seam. This is a real change of hull plane, never a diagonal beam or stripe of paint. The three cylinders keep their native line, with room to slide. Keep one large lower cargo door, one roof recess and the recognizable high faceted hump.'),
 ('M18','D03','Донорские сборки','Три крупных донорских секции','Three large closed assemblies form a coherent rebuilt freighter: an angular forward cabin-adjacent cargo shoulder, a deep central hold under the original high hump, and a compact stepped rear engine-transition section. Distinguish their broad contours and junction depths, not merely paint. No gaps into an empty hull. The middle section carries the native cylinder row on one continuous thick cheek. Each assembly gets at most one purposeful medium recess; keep large surfaces quiet.'),
 ('M19','D01','Донорские сборки','Восстановленный заводской корпус','Stay closest to the original RH factory cargo-body silhouette but show a substantial expert scrapyard rebuild through ONE replaced lower hull corner and an enlarged angular closed hatch housing integrated into the rear shoulder. Use broad crisp cuts, real recessed returns and a repaired large panel boundary. Original high hump, compact engines and original cabin are clearly recognizable. Keep one deep cargo panel and one moderate vent; avoid the D-series dense repeated fields and nested bezels.'),
 ('M20','D05','Донорские сборки','Собранный силуэт','A balanced lead candidate combining a strong front sloped closed shoulder, one continuous midlevel drum cheek and a deep but concise lower cargo-door recess. The rear shoulder has a single substantial step into each original-sized engine collar. Keep the narrow high faceted original hump with one roof service field and an uncluttered side face. One large dark-red donor casing and one pale replacement corner have physically distinct cuts. Cohesive angular silhouette, moderate relief, about half the D-series subdivision; no little decorative attachments.'),
]

def sha(p):return hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest()

def main():
    prev=TOPIC/'rh_freighter/concepts/round_07'
    old=json.loads((prev/'prompts.json').read_text(encoding='utf8'))
    refs=[Path(old['references'][0]['path']),Path(old['references'][1]['path']),
          TOPIC/'rh_elite/prototypes/concept_b/uv_refinement_01/after_bottom_corner.png',
          Path('H:/FL/EXP/EXTRA_ART/RH/R - Freighter.jpg')]
    variants=[]
    for label,base,group,title,direction in VARIANTS:
        target=prev/f'rh_freighter_{base}_sheet.png'
        variants.append(dict(id=label,label=label,base_variant=base,title_ru=title,group_ru=group,
                             target_image=target.as_posix(),target_sha256=sha(target),
                             prompt=COMMON+direction+'\nMain title exactly: "RH JUNKER FREIGHTER / '+label+'".'))
    assert len(variants)==20
    payload=dict(tool='built-in image_gen',target='rh_freighter_pir',
                 latest_brief='TWENTY new distinct concise angular hull variants; about HALF the detail density of D01-D05; preserve original freighter cabin/scale/high hump/six sliding side drums; no exposed pipes or exterior frames; implemented RH elite construction quality.',
                 source_cmp=old['source_cmp'],source_sha256=old['source_sha256'],
                 native_flight_dimensions_xyz=old['native_flight_dimensions_xyz'],
                 elite_cmp_reference=old['elite_cmp'],elite_sha256_at_previous_review=old['elite_sha256'],
                 references=[dict(path=p.as_posix(),sha256=sha(p)) for p in refs],variants=variants)
    OUT.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True)
    path=OUT/'prompts.json'
    if path.exists():raise FileExistsError(path)
    path.write_text(json.dumps(payload,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2),encoding='utf8')
    print(path)

if __name__=='__main__':main()
